        "math_academy_id": "q-317169",
        "knowledge_point_id": "798800a5-255a-5738-91d2-21c2a78e4fb5",
        "knowledge_point": "Identifying Quadrants Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table",
        "problem": "Consider the expression $f(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{5}.$ Fill the table with appropriate sign of $f(x,y)$ as shown below.\n\n| $y>0$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |",
        "worked_solution": "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038247/assets/5fd85410238231152b6f905921f594af15293d6efc54b0b1ce558131a05bf6ee.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038247/assets/e95107f16eb80a06cd172c30ec78992838f974a91f9f2fa17b7c8ac77125c438.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | ${x}^{3}$ | ${y}^{5}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038247/assets/36e292eea1cf0be1527b2a357f1939e4c95f0202aa0ce5b91e9079da493f2155.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |",
