        "knowledge_point_id": "5dc0250e-2aa9-5439-b567-93a59fa1c364",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding the Coefficient of a Term In the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial of a Given Function",
        "problem": "What is the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-2}$ about $x=0?$",
        "worked_solution": "The coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{(3)}(0).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\frac{6}{(x-2)^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{‴}(0),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{‴}(0)=-\\frac{6}{(0-2)^{4}}=-\\frac{6}{16}=-\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(0)=\\frac{1}{3!}\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{8})=-\\frac{3}{8\\cdot 6}=-\\frac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\nSo the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is $-\\frac{1}{16}.$",
