        "knowledge_point": "Identifying When a Rational Function has No Horizontal Asymptote",
        "problem": "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{5+7{x}^{3}}{12x}.$",
        "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5+7{x}^{3}}{12x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{x})}{(\\frac{12x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5}{x}+7{x}^{2})}{12} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(0+7{x}^{2})}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote.",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
            "key": "selection",
            "type": "radio",
            "choices_complete": true,
            "choices": [
